1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:00,980
Hey, everyone.

2
00:00:00,980 --> 00:00:04,029
We're going to be taking a look at two actions that are ...

3
00:00:04,029 --> 00:00:07,078
actually costly and people pursue them all the time.

4
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:10,040
These are our sunk and hidden cost fallacies.

5
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:11,640
Let's jump into it.

6
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,813
Have you ever forced yourself to sit through a terrible movie ...

7
00:00:14,813 --> 00:00:16,799
just because you paid for the movie ticket?

8
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:19,698
Or spent hours trying to fix something when starting over ...

9
00:00:19,698 --> 00:00:20,839
would have been faster?

10
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:23,374
You're not alone, and you may have fallen into ...

11
00:00:23,374 --> 00:00:26,296
one of the two common traps, the sunk cost fallacy or the ...

12
00:00:26,296 --> 00:00:27,439
hidden cost fallacy.

13
00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:30,133
These mental pitfalls affect our personal lives,

14
00:00:30,133 --> 00:00:31,910
our work, and even major corporate ...

15
00:00:31,910 --> 00:00:33,319
and government decisions.

16
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:36,401
Here we're going to look at how they are different and ...

17
00:00:36,401 --> 00:00:37,679
how you can avoid them.

18
00:00:38,480 --> 00:00:40,120
Let's start with the basics.

19
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:43,112
Sunk cost is anything you've already spent money,

20
00:00:43,112 --> 00:00:45,400
time, or effort that you can't get back.

21
00:00:45,520 --> 00:00:48,679
The sunk cost fallacy happens when we let those ...

22
00:00:48,679 --> 00:00:52,162
past investments influence our future decisions,

23
00:00:52,162 --> 00:00:55,078
even when doing so makes no rational sense.

24
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:58,015
In other words, just because you've already ...

25
00:00:58,015 --> 00:01:00,145
spent, it doesn't mean you should ...

26
00:01:00,145 --> 00:01:01,438
keep spending more.

27
00:01:02,440 --> 00:01:04,262
Here, do you want to continue to ...

28
00:01:04,262 --> 00:01:06,645
watching that movie that you don't like,

29
00:01:06,645 --> 00:01:08,678
or do you want to do something else?

30
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:11,640
We should ignore what we've already spent.

31
00:01:12,120 --> 00:01:14,680
Now contrast that with a hidden cost.

32
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:16,760
Hidden cost are not about the past.

33
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:19,720
They're about the cost we don't immediately notice or measure.

34
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:22,445
And when we fall into the hidden cost fallacy,

35
00:01:22,445 --> 00:01:25,439
we ignore those irrelevant costs in our decisions.

36
00:01:25,640 --> 00:01:27,418
So here they may be emotional stress,

37
00:01:27,418 --> 00:01:28,737
time loss, mental fatigue,

38
00:01:28,737 --> 00:01:29,999
or missed opportunities.

39
00:01:30,120 --> 00:01:33,577
They sneak up on us and often turn a seemingly cheap ...

40
00:01:33,577 --> 00:01:35,799
decision into an expensive one.

41
00:01:35,880 --> 00:01:38,255
In the long run, let's think about the coffee ...

42
00:01:38,255 --> 00:01:41,079
shop that wants to add alcohol beverages to the menu.

43
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:42,920
What takes place here?

44
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:45,401
Well, if we have a bunch of people ...

45
00:01:45,401 --> 00:01:49,269
who work in our coffee shop and they're under the age of ...

46
00:01:49,269 --> 00:01:51,246
21, then we might run into an ...

47
00:01:51,246 --> 00:01:54,942
issue with a legal issue in which we have to hire more ...

48
00:01:54,942 --> 00:01:58,724
people who are of legal age that can serve and pour the ...

49
00:01:58,724 --> 00:02:00,357
alcoholic beverages.

50
00:02:00,440 --> 00:02:04,960
So that's an additional cost that we did not consider initially.

51
00:02:05,360 --> 00:02:07,085
Now, let's think about the common ...

52
00:02:07,085 --> 00:02:09,119
confusion that arises between the two.

53
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:10,480
Here's where it gets tricky.

54
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:12,800
People often mix up these.

55
00:02:12,960 --> 00:02:14,160
Let's take an example.

56
00:02:14,240 --> 00:02:17,278
You've been on hold with customer service for three ...

57
00:02:17,278 --> 00:02:18,479
hours. That's time.

58
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:20,400
It's a sunk cost.  It's gone.

59
00:02:20,440 --> 00:02:21,720
You're never going to get it back.

60
00:02:21,880 --> 00:02:25,200
But now you're wondering, do I keep waiting?

61
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:27,640
That's where the hidden cost comes in.

62
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:31,440
The cost of waiting longer, maybe another hour, maybe two.

63
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:34,311
It's hidden to us, it's not just your time,

64
00:02:34,311 --> 00:02:36,119
but your energy, your mood.

65
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:38,440
Maybe even missing a meeting or dinner.

66
00:02:38,520 --> 00:02:41,735
People often stay in these situations because they don't ...

67
00:02:41,735 --> 00:02:44,079
want to waste what they've already spent.

68
00:02:44,240 --> 00:02:47,189
But the smarter move is to cut your losses and protect ...

69
00:02:47,189 --> 00:02:48,999
your future time and resources.

70
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:50,280
The rule is simple.

71
00:02:50,400 --> 00:02:53,360
Don't throw good time or money after bad.

72
00:02:53,760 --> 00:02:56,000
Let's turn to some real world applications.

73
00:02:56,120 --> 00:02:57,864
First, let's look at the sunk cost ...

74
00:02:57,864 --> 00:02:59,359
fallacy in the Concorde jet.

75
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:01,342
Here, the Concorde supersonic jet ...

76
00:03:01,342 --> 00:03:03,919
was built by the British and French governments.

77
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,320
It was an ambitious and expensive project.

78
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:10,560
But even after it was clear the jet would never be profitable,

79
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:13,040
they kept pouring money into it. Why?

80
00:03:13,120 --> 00:03:14,800
Because it already spent billions.

81
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:17,240
That's the textbook sunk cost fallacy.

82
00:03:17,320 --> 00:03:20,027
Continuing a failing investment just because ...

83
00:03:20,027 --> 00:03:22,039
you've already invested heavily.

84
00:03:22,240 --> 00:03:24,768
If they had asked what's the best use of our money going ...

85
00:03:24,768 --> 00:03:26,284
forward, they likely would have ...

86
00:03:26,284 --> 00:03:27,239
canceled it sooner.

87
00:03:27,880 --> 00:03:29,612
The Hidden Cost Fallacy Here,

88
00:03:29,612 --> 00:03:31,759
we're going to look at cheap airfare.

89
00:03:31,880 --> 00:03:35,120
You see a flight for $200 looks like a great deal, right?

90
00:03:35,480 --> 00:03:36,880
But here's the catch.

91
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:39,141
12 hour layover in the middle of the night,

92
00:03:39,141 --> 00:03:41,322
stuck in the airport, running on no sleep,

93
00:03:41,322 --> 00:03:42,879
wasting your time and energy.

94
00:03:43,040 --> 00:03:45,546
And when you finally arrive, you're exhausted,

95
00:03:45,546 --> 00:03:48,679
behind schedule, and your productivity for the day is shot.

96
00:03:48,840 --> 00:03:50,520
Sure, the ticket was cheap.

97
00:03:50,680 --> 00:03:55,640
But the hidden cost lost time, lost energy, lost comfort.

98
00:03:55,840 --> 00:03:58,128
When you only look at a price tag,

99
00:03:58,128 --> 00:04:00,839
you miss the full cost of your choices.

100
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:03,080
So what's our lesson?

101
00:04:03,240 --> 00:04:06,520
When making decisions, always look forward, not backward.

102
00:04:06,800 --> 00:04:09,760
The money, time and effort you've already spent is gone.

103
00:04:09,840 --> 00:04:11,200
That's the sunk cost.

104
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:14,901
Don't let it trap you into continuing something that no ...

105
00:04:14,901 --> 00:04:16,119
longer serves you.

106
00:04:16,240 --> 00:04:18,527
And don't forget to look for the hidden costs,

107
00:04:18,527 --> 00:04:21,355
those sneaky consequences that don't show up in the fine ...

108
00:04:21,355 --> 00:04:22,679
print but hit your wallet.

109
00:04:22,760 --> 00:04:24,600
Your time and your well being.

110
00:04:24,640 --> 00:04:27,225
Later on, being aware of both of these ...

111
00:04:27,225 --> 00:04:29,810
fallacies can help you make smarter,

112
00:04:29,810 --> 00:04:31,478
more rational choices.

113
00:04:31,520 --> 00:04:34,338
Whether you're managing a budget or running a ...

114
00:04:34,338 --> 00:04:36,785
business, or just deciding how to spend ...

115
00:04:36,785 --> 00:04:39,158
your weekend, make decisions based on ...

116
00:04:39,158 --> 00:04:42,198
what's still possible, not what's already lost.

117
00:04:42,680 --> 00:04:44,680
Hope you've enjoyed it.  We'll catch you in our next one.
